00:08:29.653 --> 00:08:31.570
MAX SALTONSTALL: So two
really big challenges.

00:08:31.570 --> 00:08:33.028
You've got a latency
challenge; you

00:08:33.028 --> 00:08:36.080
have to make a lot of
responses very, very quickly.

00:08:36.080 --> 00:08:37.929
And you have a big
data challenge;

00:08:37.929 --> 00:08:41.350
creating and processing
all of that data every day

00:08:41.350 --> 00:08:43.570
for your data science and
machine learning pipelines.

00:08:43.570 --> 00:08:44.830
Wow.

00:08:44.830 --> 00:08:47.380
I am really excited to
dive into the architecture

00:08:47.380 --> 00:08:48.440
of all of this.

00:08:48.440 --> 00:08:51.160
How did you make the
magic of ad serving

00:08:51.160 --> 00:08:54.940
happen with all of that
data on top of Google Cloud?

00:08:54.940 --> 00:08:57.880
CHANG KIM: We see advertisement
campaign commissions

00:08:57.880 --> 00:09:02.560
from our advertisers directly
through the front end

00:09:02.560 --> 00:09:04.060
or through API.

00:09:04.060 --> 00:09:05.770
We receive their
request and then

00:09:05.770 --> 00:09:09.280
we create the internally this
thing called campaign data

00:09:09.280 --> 00:09:12.880
structures, along with
the actual creatives

00:09:12.880 --> 00:09:16.540
that the customer has
created to show their end

00:09:16.540 --> 00:09:18.970
customers or the device users.

00:09:18.970 --> 00:09:23.590
And creatives are really the
image files or video files

00:09:23.590 --> 00:09:27.260
or the texts that are shown
as part of the advertisement,

00:09:27.260 --> 00:09:27.760
right?

00:09:27.760 --> 00:09:31.270
So this builds the core
campaign structure.

00:09:31.270 --> 00:09:34.600
Then these are
stored on Datastore,

00:09:34.600 --> 00:09:38.110
Google managed Datastore
system because this

00:09:38.110 --> 00:09:41.420
requires SQLite access as well.

00:09:41.420 --> 00:09:44.860
And then on the
other end, we keep

00:09:44.860 --> 00:09:48.010
receiving these bid requests,
huge number of bid requests,

00:09:48.010 --> 00:09:51.250
through the ad exchanges so
we have a dedicated system

00:09:51.250 --> 00:09:54.190
called the bid processor.

00:09:54.190 --> 00:09:58.120
It's running on top of
GKE and then at the peak

00:09:58.120 --> 00:10:03.130
we run, I don't know, thousands
of containers per region

00:10:03.130 --> 00:10:07.490
to keep sustaining
this input rate.

00:10:07.490 --> 00:10:09.580
So when we receive
this bid processor,

00:10:09.580 --> 00:10:14.470
each bid we then need to
choose a few campaigns

00:10:14.470 --> 00:10:16.360
or a large number
of campaigns that we

00:10:16.360 --> 00:10:18.040
need to evaluate against.

00:10:18.040 --> 00:10:21.130
Basically we need to
make a decision, OK,

00:10:21.130 --> 00:10:26.140
for this bid request, basically
for this particular user using

00:10:26.140 --> 00:10:28.150
this device at
this point in time

00:10:28.150 --> 00:10:32.890
with this kind of content and
context, which of our campaigns

00:10:32.890 --> 00:10:34.210
are good matches?

00:10:34.210 --> 00:10:37.090
So we have to do that
prediction using machine

00:10:37.090 --> 00:10:38.440
learning technologies.

00:10:38.440 --> 00:10:42.520
So that's done through
our custom TensorFlow

00:10:42.520 --> 00:10:47.650
serving infrastructure running
on top of the GKE containers

00:10:47.650 --> 00:10:48.940
as well.

00:10:48.940 --> 00:10:53.470
Then when you run this
model inference, of course

00:10:53.470 --> 00:10:55.540
you need to extract
a lot of data

00:10:55.540 --> 00:10:59.350
about the user and
then the current status

00:10:59.350 --> 00:11:02.140
of the user, which we
call context information.

00:11:02.140 --> 00:11:07.270
And the user's profile,
that's all public information

00:11:07.270 --> 00:11:10.150
collected through
various channels.

00:11:10.150 --> 00:11:13.030
And then our previous
bid history--

00:11:13.030 --> 00:11:14.980
because the chances
that we might

00:11:14.980 --> 00:11:18.050
have served this user
for our campaigns many,

00:11:18.050 --> 00:11:20.290
many, many times
before, so we wanted

00:11:20.290 --> 00:11:23.630
to learn from our previous
bidding histories as well.

00:11:23.630 --> 00:11:26.740
So we collect all
this data stored

00:11:26.740 --> 00:11:28.870
in BigTable and BigQuery.

00:11:28.870 --> 00:11:33.190
So we read these things out from
BigTable and BigQuery very fast

00:11:33.190 --> 00:11:35.560
and then handle some
of these things,

00:11:35.560 --> 00:11:39.370
process them, and then turn
them into the right feature set

00:11:39.370 --> 00:11:41.890
for machine learning
models and then feed them

00:11:41.890 --> 00:11:44.240
into the TensorFlow serving.

00:11:44.240 --> 00:11:44.740
OK?

00:11:44.740 --> 00:11:46.970
So that's what's
happening at the bid time.

00:11:46.970 --> 00:11:50.620
And then when we decide to
respond to the bid request,

00:11:50.620 --> 00:11:53.170
we generate a bid response
and then send it back

00:11:53.170 --> 00:11:56.070
to the ad exchanges.

00:11:56.070 --> 00:11:59.130
Now when we win
this, the exchanges

00:11:59.130 --> 00:12:01.860
let us know that, OK,
now you won this ad spot.

00:12:01.860 --> 00:12:04.450
Why don't you actually
show the result?

00:12:04.450 --> 00:12:07.650
So the result is shown
through the CDN service.

00:12:07.650 --> 00:12:10.980
And then when the user,
the mobile phone user

00:12:10.980 --> 00:12:14.340
actually responds to
this, the response

00:12:14.340 --> 00:12:17.040
signals also come
directly sometimes to us

00:12:17.040 --> 00:12:19.890
or sometimes through these
umpires, as I mentioned,

00:12:19.890 --> 00:12:21.150
the MMPs.

00:12:21.150 --> 00:12:24.240
So these impression
signals, click signals,

00:12:24.240 --> 00:12:28.350
and further engagement
signals are collected and then

00:12:28.350 --> 00:12:31.920
come back to us and then we
collect all these signals

00:12:31.920 --> 00:12:34.920
through our notification
processors and event

00:12:34.920 --> 00:12:36.610
processors.

00:12:36.610 --> 00:12:38.640
These are important
not just because we

00:12:38.640 --> 00:12:41.940
need to properly
generate our billing

00:12:41.940 --> 00:12:44.850
record for our
customers, but also

00:12:44.850 --> 00:12:48.930
to collect the data so that we
can keep training our models.

00:12:48.930 --> 00:12:51.750
All these transactions
that we do

00:12:51.750 --> 00:12:54.930
are just great training
examples for our models,

00:12:54.930 --> 00:13:00.360
so that's really, really
critical infrastructure, again,

00:13:00.360 --> 00:13:02.040
collecting this
feedback, then we

00:13:02.040 --> 00:13:05.110
have to keep these
things in the same place

00:13:05.110 --> 00:13:07.740
so we use BigTable again
a lot and then BigQuery

00:13:07.740 --> 00:13:08.390
a lot for that.

00:13:08.390 --> 00:13:10.890
MAX SALTONSTALL: So it seems
like that bit processor is sure

00:13:10.890 --> 00:13:12.550
doing a lot of work.

00:13:12.550 --> 00:13:15.430
How big is the Kubernetes
cluster that you need to run

00:13:15.430 --> 00:13:16.860
and how do you make it scale?

00:13:16.860 --> 00:13:17.902
CHANG KIM: Good question.

00:13:17.902 --> 00:13:20.100
The short answer is we're
relying-- finally now

00:13:20.100 --> 00:13:25.590
just relying on purely just
GKE scaling feature set.

00:13:25.590 --> 00:13:29.313
Our bid processor is the
biggest set of containers.

00:13:29.313 --> 00:13:31.230
Of course, we have many
others, but let's just

00:13:31.230 --> 00:13:33.690
talk about the bid
processor alone.

00:13:33.690 --> 00:13:38.790
It is using about two and a
half K nodes at the peak time

00:13:38.790 --> 00:13:42.020
for processing
incoming bid requests

00:13:42.020 --> 00:13:46.200
and it keeps increasing
our business grows.

00:13:46.200 --> 00:13:51.120
Then initially we started off
with our in-house scalar system

00:13:51.120 --> 00:13:52.890
to manage the
cluster for a while,

00:13:52.890 --> 00:13:58.680
but then recently we
evaluated GKEs native scalars,

00:13:58.680 --> 00:14:02.190
a few of them, and then realize
that just relying on strictly

00:14:02.190 --> 00:14:04.080
those is actually better now.

00:14:04.080 --> 00:14:07.530
So we're using just horizontal
pod scalar and cluster

00:14:07.530 --> 00:14:09.883
autoscaler offered by GCP now.

00:14:09.883 --> 00:14:12.300
MAX SALTONSTALL: OK, let's
talk about the data little bit.

00:14:12.300 --> 00:14:16.470
You said 170 terabytes
being generated every day.

00:14:16.470 --> 00:14:19.030
How do you store
that, process that,

00:14:19.030 --> 00:14:21.340
use it for your
training and analysis?

00:14:21.340 --> 00:14:25.350
CHANG KIM: So as I said, we keep
receiving these bid requests

00:14:25.350 --> 00:14:29.940
and then all the follow up
signals from the ad exchanges

00:14:29.940 --> 00:14:32.490
about our bid results
and also directly

00:14:32.490 --> 00:14:37.720
from the mobile devices about
the impressions and so on.

00:14:37.720 --> 00:14:40.920
So all this huge amount
of data arrived, again,

00:14:40.920 --> 00:14:46.410
at our infrastructure
through GCPs load balancer

00:14:46.410 --> 00:14:50.850
and then we store them
in BigTable, sometimes

00:14:50.850 --> 00:14:53.220
the raw data and GCS
because sometimes we

00:14:53.220 --> 00:14:54.690
have to replay them.

00:14:54.690 --> 00:14:57.180
And then if it's very
structured and then

00:14:57.180 --> 00:15:01.140
if it needs to be handled
by our data scientists,

00:15:01.140 --> 00:15:06.270
we also store some of
them in BigQuery as well.

00:15:06.270 --> 00:15:09.990
So we're probably one of the
biggest BigTable users of GCP

00:15:09.990 --> 00:15:15.330
right now and all these
raw data are stored here

00:15:15.330 --> 00:15:20.970
scattered at various platforms,
and then we read them out

00:15:20.970 --> 00:15:22.090
for two purposes.

00:15:22.090 --> 00:15:26.140
One for training and second
for analysis by human.

00:15:26.140 --> 00:15:26.640
OK?

00:15:26.640 --> 00:15:29.640
So for training, we actually
created a huge number

00:15:29.640 --> 00:15:33.630
of data flow jobs so that we can
read these things out and then

00:15:33.630 --> 00:15:38.490
turn them into the machine
learning feature sets, which

00:15:38.490 --> 00:15:44.640
we then feed into our
Kubeflow running on our VMs.

00:15:44.640 --> 00:15:48.180
And then these are actually
finally getting into--

00:15:48.180 --> 00:15:53.190
when we finish
training our models,

00:15:53.190 --> 00:15:56.070
some models are trained
hourly, some models daily,

00:15:56.070 --> 00:15:59.220
some models a little
less frequently,

00:15:59.220 --> 00:16:03.180
but whenever we end up
finishing our models properly,

00:16:03.180 --> 00:16:06.870
then we load them onto
our custom TensorFlow

00:16:06.870 --> 00:16:09.690
serving infrastructure
which is integrated

00:16:09.690 --> 00:16:12.280
into our online
bidding infrastructure,

00:16:12.280 --> 00:16:14.020
which I already explained.

00:16:14.020 --> 00:16:16.920
So that's one data flow part.

00:16:16.920 --> 00:16:21.660
And then the other part, our
engineers and data scientists

00:16:21.660 --> 00:16:24.390
continuously need
to look up this data

00:16:24.390 --> 00:16:27.060
to see whether we're heading in
the right direction, whether we

00:16:27.060 --> 00:16:29.820
can improve something,
or whether the market

00:16:29.820 --> 00:16:33.930
situation-- the macro situation
have changed significantly

00:16:33.930 --> 00:16:38.220
that warrants our model update
or significant model redesigns

00:16:38.220 --> 00:16:40.740
or insertion of new
models and so on.

00:16:40.740 --> 00:16:42.870
So that pipeline
is typically done

00:16:42.870 --> 00:16:47.730
through the online
analysis tools.

00:16:47.730 --> 00:16:51.370
We're a big user of the
Looker system as well.

00:16:51.370 --> 00:16:54.000
So Looker running
on top of BigQuery

00:16:54.000 --> 00:16:57.203
is a big data processing
pipeline for us as well.

00:16:57.203 --> 00:16:58.620
MAX SALTONSTALL:
And tell me more,

00:16:58.620 --> 00:17:00.720
how is data flow
helping you move

00:17:00.720 --> 00:17:02.880
all of this data around
given the immense scale

00:17:02.880 --> 00:17:03.833
of your platform?

00:17:03.833 --> 00:17:04.500
CHANG KIM: Yeah.

00:17:04.500 --> 00:17:08.369
Data flow really reads data
out of BigTable, BigQuery,

00:17:08.369 --> 00:17:12.390
and Google Cloud Storage,
and then transforms them

00:17:12.390 --> 00:17:15.599
into feature sets.

00:17:15.599 --> 00:17:18.569
The easy integration with
BigTable and BigQuery

00:17:18.569 --> 00:17:20.670
was both in terms
of region, right?

00:17:20.670 --> 00:17:26.910
That was a big motivator
for us to try this out.

00:17:26.910 --> 00:17:32.100
We run over 1,300 data
flow jobs every day

00:17:32.100 --> 00:17:35.970
and then process, as
I said, more than 170

00:17:35.970 --> 00:17:39.270
terabyte every single day.

00:17:39.270 --> 00:17:44.070
And the largest data flow job
that we have currently uses

00:17:44.070 --> 00:17:50.040
up to about 7,000
virtual CPU hour per job,

00:17:50.040 --> 00:17:53.970
and so that's very
CPU intensive.

00:17:53.970 --> 00:17:55.860
We also use data
flow service space

00:17:55.860 --> 00:17:59.310
shuffling a lot for performance
gains these days too.

00:17:59.310 --> 00:18:02.070
MAX SALTONSTALL: What led you
to choose BigTable for storage?

00:18:02.070 --> 00:18:05.820
CHANG KIM: It gives a very
good latency and throughput

00:18:05.820 --> 00:18:06.720
at the same time.

00:18:06.720 --> 00:18:13.350
The key value region writes
over 10,000 QPS at less than--

00:18:13.350 --> 00:18:16.390
I didn't know, or 6 or 7
millisecond when we use,

00:18:16.390 --> 00:18:18.180
especially SSD nodes.

00:18:18.180 --> 00:18:21.960
That's just so fast
and scalable as well.

00:18:21.960 --> 00:18:27.300
We're handling about 220
megabytes per second.

00:18:27.300 --> 00:18:30.810
The throughput is
also great and then

00:18:30.810 --> 00:18:35.880
the embedded garbage
collection system or capability

00:18:35.880 --> 00:18:38.460
is also quite helpful because
we can just automatically

00:18:38.460 --> 00:18:41.970
forget all data without
worrying about or purging

00:18:41.970 --> 00:18:43.320
any of those things.

00:18:43.320 --> 00:18:44.778
MAX SALTONSTALL:
That is definitely

00:18:44.778 --> 00:18:47.590
a lot of data processing and
a large number of requests.

00:18:47.590 --> 00:18:49.560
So how do you
optimize all of this

00:18:49.560 --> 00:18:51.420
so that you can keep
costs under control?

00:18:51.420 --> 00:18:53.550
CHANG KIM: The challenge
is that our scale keeps

00:18:53.550 --> 00:18:56.520
growing significantly
and yet, your OPEX

00:18:56.520 --> 00:18:57.930
needs to be contained.

00:18:57.930 --> 00:19:01.440
So for example, last
two years our scale

00:19:01.440 --> 00:19:03.990
has grown more than 3x.

00:19:03.990 --> 00:19:06.210
So in terms of number
of bid requests

00:19:06.210 --> 00:19:09.120
that we receive as well as
the number of bid responses

00:19:09.120 --> 00:19:13.380
that we generate,
both more than 3x.

00:19:13.380 --> 00:19:15.360
So in terms of bid
responses we're

00:19:15.360 --> 00:19:22.170
now handling or generating
more than 700 billion responses

00:19:22.170 --> 00:19:25.740
every single month.

00:19:25.740 --> 00:19:29.140
But we track, for
example, our unit cost.

00:19:29.140 --> 00:19:36.600
So for example, with one unit
dollar, how many bid responses

00:19:36.600 --> 00:19:37.950
are we able to generate?

00:19:37.950 --> 00:19:40.200
That's, for example,
one metric that we

00:19:40.200 --> 00:19:42.600
use to measure our efficiency.

00:19:42.600 --> 00:19:50.700
So that cost efficiency has been
slightly going up last year,

00:19:50.700 --> 00:19:53.620
but then we realized that,
well, we had to contain that.

00:19:53.620 --> 00:19:57.420
So we actually decide to spend
a lot of engineering resources,

00:19:57.420 --> 00:20:01.380
optimization resources by
taking advantage of a lot of GCP

00:20:01.380 --> 00:20:03.790
capabilities as well.

00:20:03.790 --> 00:20:07.110
So it has started
to come down and it

00:20:07.110 --> 00:20:09.000
looks a little more stable now.

00:20:09.000 --> 00:20:11.790
And then some of the
key technologies,

00:20:11.790 --> 00:20:15.570
GKE native scalars
are helping us

00:20:15.570 --> 00:20:19.510
a lot because we can scale
in and out very easily.

00:20:19.510 --> 00:20:24.480
And then we also now use
more diverse types of nodes,

00:20:24.480 --> 00:20:28.350
even including preemptible VMs.

00:20:28.350 --> 00:20:30.850
So those things are
all very helpful.

00:20:30.850 --> 00:20:32.100
MAX SALTONSTALL: That's great.

00:20:32.100 --> 00:20:35.790
So why did you end up choosing
Google Cloud for this platform

00:20:35.790 --> 00:20:36.540
that you built?

00:20:36.540 --> 00:20:38.550
CHANG KIM: Pay as
you go pricing helped

00:20:38.550 --> 00:20:40.410
us bootstrap our business.

00:20:40.410 --> 00:20:42.510
Even when we were
very, very small,

00:20:42.510 --> 00:20:47.580
we were able to start growing
our business very easily

00:20:47.580 --> 00:20:51.300
by just simply writing on
top of very big and reliable

00:20:51.300 --> 00:20:52.710
general purpose cloud.

00:20:52.710 --> 00:20:58.140
Operationally we also couldn't
afford to have a big ops team.

00:20:58.140 --> 00:21:01.230
Our dev team had to
just focus on delivering

00:21:01.230 --> 00:21:04.710
the right infrastructure code
and then the right ML models

00:21:04.710 --> 00:21:06.890
and serving improv,
training improv,

00:21:06.890 --> 00:21:10.740
so we started off with
very lean operations team.

00:21:10.740 --> 00:21:16.230
Now we are a little bigger, but
I would say that more than 80%,

00:21:16.230 --> 00:21:20.850
70% of our operations are
offloaded to the GCP team

00:21:20.850 --> 00:21:23.850
thanks to their capabilities
and responsiveness,

00:21:23.850 --> 00:21:28.020
so that's always a big help.

00:21:28.020 --> 00:21:31.770
Stack Overflow is very helpful
as well because GCP engineers

00:21:31.770 --> 00:21:32.910
regularly monitor it.

00:21:35.726 --> 00:21:39.270
And then there are a few other
specific things that we really

00:21:39.270 --> 00:21:43.350
enjoyed on top of GCP, like
data flow service space

00:21:43.350 --> 00:21:49.230
shuffling and BigTable's
latency optimization

00:21:49.230 --> 00:21:54.990
using SSDs, and then
BigQuery SQL performance

00:21:54.990 --> 00:21:59.970
improvement over time, then
integration with Looker and BI.

00:21:59.970 --> 00:22:02.093
Those things have been
super helpful as well.

00:22:02.093 --> 00:22:03.510
MAX SALTONSTALL:
Thank you, Chang,

00:22:03.510 --> 00:22:06.630
very much for joining us
and sharing a lot of insight

00:22:06.630 --> 00:22:10.470
into how and why you built
this ad serving platform

00:22:10.470 --> 00:22:11.640
on top of Google Cloud.

00:22:11.640 --> 00:22:12.720
That is so cool.

00:22:12.720 --> 00:22:16.290
I now understand a lot
better how those ads show up

00:22:16.290 --> 00:22:19.650
on my screen, what it takes
to build a very latency

00:22:19.650 --> 00:22:23.370
sensitive, high performance
ads platform on Google Cloud,

00:22:23.370 --> 00:22:26.820
and maybe a little bit more
about the immense power of some

00:22:26.820 --> 00:22:28.380
of these tools.

00:22:28.380 --> 00:22:30.865
If you liked what you saw,
please like and subscribe,

00:22:30.865 --> 00:22:32.490
and check out the
playlist Architecting

00:22:32.490 --> 00:22:34.710
with Google Cloud for
more videos like these.

00:22:34.710 --> 00:22:36.890
Thanks for joining us.

